  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The selected text is the complete Rhythmus S. Thomae Aquinatis printed among the thanksgiving prayers after Mass in the 1962 Missal: seven four-line strophes and Amen, 149 words. Each printed verse line is one verse segment, so the 28 segments keep the rhythmic line, the printed stanza grouping by fours and the address of each line; the closing Amen stays inside the last segment, as in the other devotional texts of this corpus whose Amen closes the same speaker's final sentence. One singular worshipper speaks throughout and addresses Christ present in the Sacrament. QUAE ... LATITAS is a relative clause in the second person whose antecedent is the addressed DEITAS. In the second strophe VISUS, TACTUS and GUSTUS are nominative subjects of the singular personal passive FALLITUR; CREDITUR is an impersonal passive whose means is AUDITU SOLO, and TUTO is the adverb. NIL ... VERIUS is a verbless comparison: VERIUS agrees with NIL and takes the ablative of comparison HOC VERBO, on which VERITATIS depends. AMBO is a neuter plural object of the participles CREDENS and CONFITENS, which agree with the speaker of PETO; QUOD PETIVIT LATRO is a relative clause with an understood antecedent. FAC governs the accusative ME with the three infinitives CREDERE, HABERE and DILIGERE. PANIS VIVUS is a nominative of address beside the vocative MEMORIALE. PRAESTA governs the dative MENTI as the one granted to live, and an accusative-and-infinitive TE ... SAPERE with ILLI as dative and DULCE as an adverbial neuter. QUIT, from QUEO, governs FACERE, whose object is TOTUM MUNDUM with the predicative SALVUM. FIAT ILLUD is a jussive subjunctive whose subject ILLUD is defined by the relative QUOD, the object of the transitive SITIO. In the last strophe TE is the object of CERNENS, REVELATA FACIE is an ablative absolute, and CERNENS and BEATUS agree with the speaker of SIM; VISU is an ablative with the objective genitive TUAE GLORIAE. The heading, the indulgence grant, the neighbouring thanksgiving prayers, chant notation and stanza numbers are not part of the spoken text.",
    "source": {
      "method": "Transcribed from the directly viewed controlling page of the Benziger 1962 Editio iuxta typicam and collated character by character against the separately set Vatican 1962 typical-edition page, the 1952 Enchiridion indulgentiarum, the 1910 Raccolta and the 1961 Liber Usualis, each read from its page images.",
